WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.560
Daniel Calvisi, Script Doctor, Writing&nbsp;
Coach and Author: I just saw Oppenheimer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:01.560 --> 00:00:07.020
and Christopher Nolan has a lot of&nbsp;
expositional and explanatory dialogue…

00:00:07.020 --> 00:00:10.320
Film Courage: What is on-the-nose dialogue?&nbsp;

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:19.920
Dan: On-the-nose dialogue is dialogue that&nbsp;
is generic, it is describing something that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:27.060
we could easily see that is clear in&nbsp;
the scene. It's explaining something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:36.180
it's just kind of like lazy dialogue&nbsp;
that doesn't show that character's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:42.840
voice. Film Courage: Is there ever a time when&nbsp;
it's okay to write on-the-nose dialogue? Dan:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:50.640
Every now and then you may have a complicated&nbsp;
sequence that you want to help the reader&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:56.880
check in and kind of orient themselves so&nbsp;
you might have a character say something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:04.980
Why did you bring…I told you to bring the&nbsp;
red car? Why did you bring the blue car?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:13.920
So then that reminds the reader that Oh, they&nbsp;
switched cars back there remember that so you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:18.720
might want to plant something that is a bit&nbsp;
on-the-nose and I don't feel like a writer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:24.420
should feel the pressure to write [Aaron]&nbsp;
Sorkin-esque dialogue for every character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:29.460
every single character doesn't have have&nbsp;
to have incredibly stylized dialogue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:37.320
the show Succession does somehow they get away&nbsp;
with it every character is clever and makes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:46.020
incredible quips just off the top of their head&nbsp;
and every character has really genius put downs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.020 --> 00:01:51.660
of other people but somehow it works on that&nbsp;
show but it doesn't work in most scripts where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:56.880
you don't want your characters to talk the same&nbsp;
you definitely don't want them to have the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.880 --> 00:02:03.240
style of dialogue it can it be done almost&nbsp;
if it's kind of like making fun of itself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:09.720
like kind of a self-reverential dialogue or&nbsp;
or almost trying to I don't even know what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:14.040
the word would be but just certain things that&nbsp;
are just I wouldn't say campy but you're trying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.040 --> 00:02:19.560
to almost make it a joke and so it's over the&nbsp;
top on-the-nose dialogue because it's sort of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:27.720
like a joke on itself I mean you could there's&nbsp;
a there's playing with the genre or winking to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.720 --> 00:02:32.880
the audience that you might want to do yeah it&nbsp;
really I mean it really depends on the material&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:39.420
sure what if you had said earlier that the&nbsp;
character should sound separate from each&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.420 --> 00:02:43.800
other what if a writer is getting the note that&nbsp;
all of their characters are sounding the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.800 --> 00:02:50.220
but they think no these are all individuals I've&nbsp;
gotten that note too it's tough because when you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.220 --> 00:02:54.780
read your own work they seem distinctive&nbsp;
but to other people sometimes they don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.460 --> 00:03:02.760
you can it might help to write down&nbsp;
back stories for your characters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:09.360
it helps to read it aloud it helps to have a&nbsp;
reading with friends I do readings over Zoom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:16.380
now so it's really easy to do and just to hear&nbsp;
it spoken out loud I think does really help&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:22.260
but I think the main thing is to know&nbsp;
your character's goals and their wants&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:31.260
in general and then also in that particular scene&nbsp;
so then that will inform how they speak if they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.260 --> 00:03:38.340
want this out of that other character they would&nbsp;
talk in this way because they're trying to maybe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.340 --> 00:03:44.100
sway the other character ideally the audience&nbsp;
is in on that too it knows their wants that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.100 --> 00:03:48.300
was something that David Mamet always said he&nbsp;
said “The only thing an actor needs to know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.300 --> 00:03:54.720
is what they want in the scene and ideally&nbsp;
the audience knows that too.” Doesn't always&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.720 --> 00:04:01.560
have to be I mean maybe you want to surprise us&nbsp;
Breaking Bad and Better Call Saul, Vince Gilligan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.700 --> 00:04:08.940
and the writers on those shows, they do a really&nbsp;
interesting style of discovery where at the end of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.940 --> 00:04:16.680
each scene comes kind of like the reason why the&nbsp;
scene existed like it's that moment of discovery&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.940 --> 00:04:24.360
that we didn't see coming and that that's truly&nbsp;
the thing that advances the story before that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.360 --> 00:04:31.380
there's just really compelling character work and&nbsp;
compelling dialogue but at the end of the scene&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.220 --> 00:04:37.620
we realize oh that's what that's what he meant&nbsp;
when he said that earlier and that's what they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.620 --> 00:04:41.820
were going for and oh I didn't see that coming&nbsp;
there's some kind of discovery that's how Walt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.820 --> 00:04:48.660
is going to get out of this trap you'll see&nbsp;
it if you watch especially Better Call Saul&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.660 --> 00:04:53.160
there's always that kind of discovery that they&nbsp;
build up to in a scene and it's really great work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.820 --> 00:05:00.060
how does the character's internal and&nbsp;
external goal factor into their dialogue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.020 --> 00:05:07.800
it's very key for their dialogue it's very&nbsp;
important the in the external goal is their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.800 --> 00:05:13.080
action goal their physical goal that they&nbsp;
need to achieve kind of like the a story goal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.580 --> 00:05:19.440
maybe it's the most obvious or most visual&nbsp;
goal and then the internal like it says it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.440 --> 00:05:25.260
going to be more emotional it might be the love&nbsp;
interest it might be their family or it might be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.260 --> 00:05:33.540
just kind of a secondary active goal but it's not&nbsp;
as important as the as the external unless later&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.540 --> 00:05:38.640
they flip and maybe the internal becomes more&nbsp;
important than the external if your character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.640 --> 00:05:44.460
is external and internal goal I think it's really&nbsp;
going to help you write your dialogue because then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.460 --> 00:05:52.500
you're writing their dialogue to show what they&nbsp;
want and to get them actively toward their goal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.180 --> 00:06:01.620
we're Ascent I mean we're essentially selfish&nbsp;
people so when we speak it's because we're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.620 --> 00:06:07.380
trying to get somewhere we're trying to get&nbsp;
something so it really helps to know their goals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.980 --> 00:06:14.040
what are your thoughts on monologues&nbsp;
and screenplays don't make them too long&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.500 --> 00:06:25.260
I mean I think maybe one or two long dialogue&nbsp;
passages are okay but for especially for new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.260 --> 00:06:33.000
writers you don't want to have super super long&nbsp;
dialogue blocks and too many monologues just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:40.680
saw Oppenheimer and Christopher Nolan has a lot of&nbsp;
expositional and explanatory dialogue but it makes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.680 --> 00:06:47.040
sense because they're all scientists and they're&nbsp;
going back and forth about all of these scientific&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.040 --> 00:06:55.020
topics and they're just giving detail after&nbsp;
detail and it's you you are okay with it more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.020 --> 00:07:02.160
because visually he's a stunning director the big&nbsp;
screen experience is important to him and I saw in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.160 --> 00:07:08.220
the theater and it is interesting to watch but on&nbsp;
the page I'm guessing there's just it would just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.220 --> 00:07:14.700
be so much long long long dialogue on-the-nose&nbsp;
dialogue scenes with so much exposition and I'd be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.700 --> 00:07:21.360
like oh my God when are they going to stop talking&nbsp;
about fission versus fusion so a newer writer who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.360 --> 00:07:26.640
doesn't have that track record who isn't known&nbsp;
for something like Aaron Sorkin might be known&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.640 --> 00:07:35.340
for monologues but you're not so you really want&nbsp;
to keep the dialogue as tight as possible maybe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.340 --> 00:07:43.140
with the a few flourishes every now and then&nbsp;
maybe you do have a key monologue once or twice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.140 --> 00:07:50.220
in your script that ideally it captures theme&nbsp;
it explores the theme what your story is about&nbsp;&nbsp;

